# CortexSim environment template.
#
# Copy this to `.env` (which is gitignored) and fill in a real secret:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Easiest path: run `scripts/dev-up.sh` — it creates `.env` for you with a
# freshly generated secret in development mode, then brings the stack up.
#
# docker-compose.yml reads these via ${VAR} interpolation.
#
# ADDING A VARIABLE HERE? Declare it on core/config.py::Settings as well, even
# when only docker-compose.yml reads it. `.env` is a superset shared by compose
# and the app, and pydantic-settings hands every dotenv key to Settings — an
# undeclared one makes `Settings()` raise, which breaks `cp .env.example .env`,
# the pytest suite, and any local boot. tests/test_config.py fails until you
# declare it. Do not relax that by setting extra="ignore": rejecting unknown
# dotenv keys is the only thing that catches a typo'd setting name.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_ENV — "development" or "production".
#
#   development  Boot proceeds with a weak/short secret (a warning is
#                logged), EXCEPT the replace-me placeholder below, which is
#                refused in development too: it is public, and the vault it
#                would encrypt is real. Use this for local labs and first-run.
#   production   The boot-time master-key guard (core/config.py
#                validate_master_key) REFUSES to start unless CORTEXSIM_SECRET
#                is high-entropy AND at least 32 bytes. This is intentional:
#                the master key encrypts every credential CortexSim stores.
#
# docker-compose.yml defaults this to "development" when unset, so a bare
# `docker compose up` does not hit the production guard. Set it to
# "production" explicitly (here or in your shell) for real deployments.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORTEXSIM_ENV=development

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_SECRET — master key that encrypts stored credentials.
#
# REQUIRED. For production it MUST be >= 32 bytes of high entropy, and must
# not be a placeholder like "changeme" / "secret" / "default" (the guard
# rejects those). Generate one with EITHER of these one-liners:
#
#     python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))'
#     openssl rand -base64 48
#
# In production, source this from a real secrets manager instead of a file:
#     export CORTEXSIM_SECRET=$(op read 'op://Private/cortexsim-master/key')
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORTEXSIM_SECRET=replace-me-with-a-generated-32plus-byte-secret

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_PORT — host port SimCore (UI + API) is published on. Default 8888.
# Health check: http://localhost:${CORTEXSIM_PORT}/api/health. It reads
# {"status":"degraded"} on every deployment by design, because api_auth
# (no per-request authentication yet) is always listed in degraded_components;
# any OTHER entry there is a real fault.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORTEXSIM_PORT=8888

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_ALLOWED_HOSTS — DNS names (beyond localhost, IP literals and the
# container's own hostname) the console, the installer one-liners and the
# beacons may use to reach SimCore. Comma list; '*.lab.example' matches
# subdomains; '*' disables the check. Anything else gets 400 HOST_NOT_ALLOWED
# (DNS-rebinding guard). Inside the container gethostname() is the container
# id, NOT the jumpbox name, so a Windows beacon started with
# --server http://JUMPBOX:8888, or a DC browsing to the jumpbox by name, is
# refused until that name is listed here. scripts/dev-up.sh seeds it with this
# host's short and fully qualified names when it generates .env.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORTEXSIM_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_BIND — host address docker-compose.yml publishes CORTEXSIM_PORT on
# (the port mapping's host_ip). Empty = every host address, IPv4 and IPv6,
# which beacons on the lab network need. 127.0.0.1 gives a console-only SimCore
# that no beacon can reach. 0.0.0.0 is IPv4 only. Write an IPv6 address without
# brackets (::1, fd00::10). SimCore reads it only to name the exposure in
# /api/health (api_auth) and the boot warning.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORTEXSIM_BIND=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_CORS_ORIGINS — comma list of origins allowed to read the API
# cross-origin. Empty (the default) mounts NO CORS middleware: the console is
# same-origin and needs none. Credentials are never allowed, even when set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORTEXSIM_CORS_ORIGINS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_TRUSTED_PROXIES — comma list of CIDRs whose X-Forwarded-For is
# believed when recording an agent's last_ip (and the run's stitched src_ip).
# Empty (the default): X-Forwarded-For is ignored and the socket peer is used.
# Behind a reverse proxy, list the proxy here, or every agent reads as the
# proxy's address.
# It is read from the process environment, not declared on core/config.py
# Settings, so leave this line COMMENTED in a .env that a host-run SimCore
# reads: an uncommented undeclared key makes Settings() refuse to boot.
# docker-compose.yml passes it through as an environment variable, which is
# safe. Export it in your shell or compose environment instead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_VERSION — single source that drives BOTH the image tag and the
# container name:
#   image:          cortex-pov-engine-simcore:<version>
#   container_name: cortex-pov-engine-simcore-v<version>
# Bump here (or `export CORTEXSIM_VERSION=…`) when you cut a release. Held in
# the cortex-pov-engine namespace on purpose — never collides with the
# separate cortex-pov-agent project.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CORTEXSIM_VERSION=1.1.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_BEACON_LOST_AFTER_SECONDS — how long an agent must be silent before
# the heartbeat sweep terminalises its in-flight run as beacon_lost (the Cortex
# agent may have killed the beacon). It gates the sweep itself, not the console's
# online/stale/offline label (30 s / 300 s, display only). The default (180)
# means a killed beacon's run reads "running" for about three minutes plus one
# 30 s sweep; the severed step's prevent expectation is held pending and can
# never read prevented.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_BEACON_LOST_AFTER_SECONDS=180

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_RECONCILE_WINDOW_ANALYTICS — match window (seconds) for Analytics /
# ABIOC expectations on every reconcile path (manual /observations, /reconcile,
# the auto sweep). The default (86400) is the largest window /reconcile accepts,
# so a slow-to-fire detector is not called missed early; the cost is that a
# genuine miss on those rows is only declared after a full day. Tighten it
# against the Cortex XDR Analytics Alert Reference Guide for the detectors a POV
# actually exercises.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_RECONCILE_WINDOW_ANALYTICS=86400

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_CANARY_SCENARIO_LINUX — scenario id POST /api/runs/canary launches as
# the block-mode canary (a benign trigger the agent is expected to terminate).
# Empty (the default) ships no canary: the endpoint returns 409
# CANARY_NOT_AVAILABLE and block mode stays operator-attested only: with no
# canary there is no evidence either way, the agent_profile_mode "block"
# attestation stands, and a silent prevent row resolves missed. The
# pending/BLOCK_MODE_UNCONFIRMED hold applies only when a launch passed a
# canary_run_id whose canary produced no prevented Result (proof the profile does
# not block). Set this if you want that guard.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORTEXSIM_CANARY_SCENARIO_LINUX=
